# SPA routes, enumerated on purpose.
#
# A catch-all `/*  /index.html  200` used to sit at the bottom of this file,
# which meant every URL on the domain answered 200 with the app shell — a soft
# 404. Agents probing for `/api`, `/docs` or `/.well-known/…` concluded that
# every path existed, and a crawler could index unlimited duplicates of the
# same shell.
#
# Listing the real routes lets everything else fall through to `404.html`,
# which Netlify serves with a genuine 404 status. `/blog/:slug` still resolves
# here; whether that *slug* exists is a database question, answered in
# netlify/edge-functions/blog-meta.ts.
#
# Adding a client route in `src/main.tsx` or `src/blog/BlogApp.tsx` means adding
# it here *and* in netlify/edge-functions/_routes.ts. `tests/routes.test.ts`
# fails when the three drift.
/                 /index.html   200
/blog             /index.html   200
/blog/*           /index.html   200
/admin            /index.html   200
/admin/*          /index.html   200

# /developers is a standalone document, not a client route — it points at its
# own file rather than the app shell. Spelled out instead of leaning on the
# host's directory-index resolution: Netlify would serve
# `developers/index.html` for `/developers` on its own, but `vite preview`
# hands back the SPA shell, and a page that only exists in production is a page
# nobody checks.
/developers       /developers/index.html   200
